Chapter: Colophon
  cites Foreword chapter FactStore
  cites Foreword chapter Sha256
  cites Foreword chapter Hamt
  cites Colophon chapter Impression

 At the back of a printed book there is a colophon: a short note saying who
 set the type, on what press, on what paper, in what month. It is the book
 describing its own making, and for five hundred years it has been the one
 page in the book that nobody could check. It says "Garamond" and you believe
 it, or you do not. It is a claim the artefact makes about itself, and the
 artefact is the only witness.

 A work stored in this repository is content-addressed, which is to say the
 work IS its hash. Change one byte of the text and it is a different work with
 a different name, and no amount of insisting otherwise will move it. So a
 colophon here can be checked: it states the hash of the work it belongs to,
 and anyone holding the work can recompute that hash and find out whether the
 colophon is lying.

 That is the whole of this chapter. It is a very old idea and a very short
 program.

Section: A Work

  Work = record {
    w-title : Text,
    w-body : Text
  }

Section: The Colophon Of A Work

 The hash is DERIVED here, never accepted. You cannot hand this function a
 hash to print, and that is deliberate: a colophon which repeats a hash it was
 given is precisely the unverifiable note we have had since Gutenberg. It takes
 the bytes and computes.

  colophon-hash : Work -> Text
  colophon-hash (w) = fact-content-hash (w.w-body)

  colophon-of : Work, Impression -> Text
  colophon-of (w) (imp) =
    "Colophon\n"
    & "  " & (w.w-title) & "\n"
    & "  " & impression-line imp & "\n"
    & "  stored under " & colophon-hash w & "\n"
    & "  " & show (text-length (w.w-body)) & " characters, and not one of them free\n"

Section: Checking One

 The question a colophon has never been able to answer: is this note about
 THIS book? Hand it the work and the note, and it will tell you. It re-derives
 the hash from the bytes in front of it and looks for that hash in the note --
 so a colophon copied from another edition, or edited after the fact, or
 simply optimistic, fails here.

  colophon-holds : Work, Text -> Boolean
  colophon-holds (w) (note) = text-contains note (colophon-hash w)

